    <span class="mono-label">GUIDE</span>
    <h1>Where do recruiters actually find first-time candidates in 2026?</h1>

    <p class="answer-capsule">Recruiters find first-time candidates mostly through referrals, university and program pipelines, skills-based talent platforms, and focused communities. Open job boards generate the most applications and the least signal, so they are usually a last resort. The strongest source is anywhere a candidate has already proven their work, because that is where recruiters can hire on evidence instead of guesswork.</p>

    <div class="tldr">
      <ul>
        <li>Referrals, program pipelines, skills-based platforms, and niche communities are where most quality entry-level hiring happens.</li>
        <li>Open job boards are high-volume and low-signal, the hardest channel for a no-experience candidate to win.</li>
        <li>Skills-based talent platforms are growing because recruiters can search on proven ability, and <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">85% of employers now hire on skills</a> (TestGorilla).</li>
        <li>The way to get found is to put evaluated work where recruiters look for proven talent.</li>
        <li>Evidence in the right place beats applications in the wrong one.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <section>
      <h2>Why are job boards the weakest source?</h2>
      <p>Job boards feel like the obvious place to look for a job, which is exactly why they work against a first-time candidate. They are open to everyone, so a single posting can draw hundreds or thousands of applications, most of which get filtered by software before a recruiter ever sees them. In that environment the recruiter is drowning in low-signal résumés and reaching for any filter that cuts the pile, which usually means pedigree and keywords. A student with no experience is competing on the weakest possible signal in the most crowded possible channel. It is the hardest game in hiring, and most students play only that game.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Where do recruiters actually look?</h2>
      <p>They look where the signal is higher and the noise is lower. Referrals are perennially one of the most trusted sources of hire, because a known person vouching for you carries real information. University and program pipelines work for the same reason: the recruiter trusts the filter the program already applied. And increasingly, recruiters use skills-based talent platforms where candidates have demonstrated ability through evaluated work, which lets them search for proven skill instead of sorting résumés. The common thread across all of these is trust. Recruiters go where someone or something has already done the work of separating signal from noise.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What is a skills-based platform, and why is it growing?</h2>
      <p>A skills-based platform inverts the job board. Instead of candidates broadcasting claims and hoping a recruiter sorts through them, candidates demonstrate ability through real, evaluated work, and recruiters search that pool for proven skill. It grows for a simple reason: it matches where hiring is already heading. With <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">85% of employers using skills-based hiring</a>, the demand is for evidence of ability, and a platform built around evaluated work supplies exactly that. For a candidate, the appeal is that your strongest asset, the quality of your work, becomes searchable, rather than buried under a résumé.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How do you get found as a student?</h2>
      <p>Stop relying on the channel where you are weakest, and build presence where recruiters look for proven talent. The practical version of this is to produce evaluated work in your target role on a platform recruiters actually use to find candidates, so your proof shows up at the point of decision. That is the model zero is built on: you take on company-shaped tasks, your work is scored against a professional bar, and recruiters pay to find candidates who have already proven they can do the job, which keeps it free for students. Getting found is less about applying harder and more about being present, with evidence, where the hiring actually happens.</p>
    </section>

